{{nihongo|'''Ichijoudani'''|一乗谷|Ichijōdani}} is the 49th episode of the 1983 [[Taisen drama]] '''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]'''. Directed by Fukui Mitsuhiro and Naito Joan, and written by Yoshihara Kaneko from a story by Kondo Masa, it aired on December 12, 1983. The episode depicts the grim reality of the prolonged siege of Ichijōdani Castle, highlighting [[Honda Shigetomo]]’s (Shinobu Otake) escalating mental fragility and the Honda army’s growing despair. The narrative intensifies the psychological horror themes of the Winter arc, culminating in a desperate act that seals the fate of both Shigetomo and his childhood friend, [[Ii Naotaka]].

== Plot ==
The episode is structured in two acts, focusing on the deteriorating siege and Shigetomo's fractured mind.

=== Act I: The Walls of Despair ===
The episode opens on the sixth week of the siege of Ichijōdani Castle. A cold, relentless rain falls, turning the battlefield into a quagmire. Honda forces are visibly exhausted, their morale at an all-time low. Food is scarce, and disease spreads through the camp. The opening sequence, directed by Fukui Mitsuhiro, uses wide, static shots of the muddy siege lines and close-ups of soldiers' gaunt faces, emphasizing the futility and suffering. Tomita Isao's score is minimal, dominated by the sounds of rain, distant drums, and the groans of the wounded.

Inside his command tent, Shigetomo is a shadow of his former self. He stares at a battle map, tracing lines with a trembling finger. His retainers, including [[Hotta Muneshige]] (Ken Takakura) and Sakata Ishiemon (Sano Yuma), present reports of dwindling supplies and increasing desertions. Each mention of the Ii clan or the name "Naotaka" triggers the "Otake Twitch"—a violent spasm under his right eye, followed by an unnerving, forced smile. He dismisses their concerns, insisting on "the law of siege," which demands unwavering persistence.

A young messenger arrives, soaked and shivering, bearing a tattered white banner from the Ii. It contains a single, defiant poem from Naotaka, mocking Shigetomo's faltering resolve. Shigetomo reads it, his face contorting with the twitch, then crushes the paper in his fist. He orders a full-scale assault at dawn, despite the rain and the army's condition, stating, "Let their defiance be their funeral pyre." Hotta and Ishiemon exchange worried glances, but dare not speak.

=== Act II: The Shadow of the River ===
The dawn assault, directed by Naito Joan, is a chaotic, desperate affair. Honda troops struggle through mud and rising river waters, attempting to breach the castle's outer defenses. The Ii defenders, though outnumbered, fight with ferocity, fueled by their lord's resolve. The battle sequence is shot in a frantic, handheld style, emphasizing the close-quarters brutality and the sheer confusion. Arrows and musket balls rain down, and many Honda soldiers drown in the swollen river.

Shigetomo watches from a nearby ridge, his face impassive yet twitching almost continuously. He sees his men fall, but his eyes seem to be fixed on something beyond the battle. In a hallucination (a recurring element from Episode 43), the ghost of his father, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Shigetada]] (Tetsuya Takeda in white makeup), appears beside him, silently pointing towards the Ichijōdani river. Shigetomo whispers to the apparition, "I tried, Father. I tried to uphold the law."

A desperate message reaches Shigetomo: the main assault has failed, with heavy casualties. Among the dead is a respected Honda officer, a veteran who had served Shigetomo's father. The news barely registers on Shigetomo's face, but the twitch intensifies. He orders the remaining siege engineers to divert the Ichijōdani river, aiming to flood the castle's foundations—a drastic and ecologically destructive measure. His generals protest, warning of the long-term consequences and the immense human cost, but Shigetomo is unyielding. "If the walls will not break, the earth will swallow them," he declares.

As night falls, the sounds of digging and the roar of the diverted river fill the air. The Ii in the castle realize Shigetomo's desperate plan. The final scene shows Shigetomo alone in his tent, his uniform splattered with mud, staring at the flowing river on his map. He picks up a small, meticulously carved wooden horse—a gift from a young Naotaka during their hostage days—and clutches it tightly. The twitch subsides, replaced by a look of profound, agonizing weariness. He knows that this act, intended to destroy his enemy, will also destroy the last vestiges of himself, irrevocably severing his bond with Naotaka and his own past. The episode ends with the ominous sounds of the river slowly rising, foreshadowing the final, tragic confrontation.

== Production ==
=== Cinematography and blocking ===

Directors Fukui Mitsuhiro and Naito Joan utilized a stark contrast between wide, static shots for the overall siege atmosphere and chaotic, handheld close-ups for battle sequences, mirroring Shigetomo's internal and external turmoil.
The use of low-angle shots and deep shadows emphasizes Shigetomo's isolation and descent into despair.
=== Sound design ===

The soundscape is dominated by natural elements: relentless rain, the churning river, and the distant, muffled sounds of a struggling army. Tomita Isao's score is sparse, using unsettling drones and a mournful biwa to underscore the psychological tension.
The "Otake Twitch" sound effect (a subtle fabric rustle) is used sparingly but effectively to punctuate Shigetomo's mental breaks.
== Historical notes ==

The diversion of rivers during sieges was a known, albeit extreme, tactic in Okaiken's Sengoku period, though its application at Ichijōdani is dramatized for the series.
The episode draws on chronicles detailing the prolonged and brutal nature of the Ichijōdani campaign, often characterized by severe weather and high casualties.

== Reception ==
"Ichijoudani" achieved a 79.60% rating, a testament to its intense dramatic portrayal, though some critics found the episode's bleakness and explicit depiction of Shigetomo's mental breakdown challenging. Viewers praised Shinobu Otake's immersive performance of Shigetomo's unraveling psyche and the atmospheric direction by Fukui Mitsuhiro and Naito Joan.

== Trivia ==

* This episode marks the heaviest use of the "Otake Twitch" throughout the series, highlighting Shigetomo's near-constant internal conflict.
* The wooden horse prop used by Shigetomo was custom-made for the production, based on descriptions of children's toys from the late Sengoku period.

